        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Incline to zero, key out, unplug, lift with your legs, latch,
            pin, pull-test.</strong> That order prevents nearly every folding problem, and the
            pull-test at the end is the step that stops a half-seated latch becoming a falling deck.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the deck <strong class="text-white">will not fold or will not latch</strong>, check the
            incline, pins, obstructions and frame bolts before blaming a part — and never store it
            folded on a latch you do not trust. For longer-term storage, see our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-store-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">storing a treadmill</a>.
